WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.380
ASP.NET Core applications should behave differently in development, test, staging, and production for good reasons.

2
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:17.800
Development environments often need detailed errors, hot reload, local credentials, permissive test settings, and diagnostic pages.

3
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:29.980
Production environments need controlled error output, stable configuration, locked-down services, secure transport expectations, and only the endpoints and features required to operate the product.

4
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.700
Configuration is not just application plumbing.

5
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.720
It is a security-relevant input.

6
00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:50.300
Connection strings, identity provider settings, allowed origins, cookie options, logging levels, feature flags, endpoint addresses, file storage paths, and dependency credentials all influence how the application behaves.

7
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:57.200
Treat configuration sources with the same seriousness as code because a weak configuration can undermine otherwise careful implementation.

8
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:04.360
Secrets should not live in source code, ordinary JSON settings files, sample configuration, screenshots, or logs.

9
00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:16.860
Development secret stores, environment-specific deployment systems, managed secret services, workload identities, and platform-provided secret mechanisms are safer places to manage sensitive values.

10
00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:25.440
The key habit is ownership: know where secrets are stored, who can read them, how they rotate, and how they are kept out of accidental disclosure paths.

11
00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.040
Middleware order is a real security concern in ASP.NET Core.

12
00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:33.100
Authentication must run before authorization.

13
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:44.960
Routing, static files, exception handling, forwarded headers, CORS, antiforgery-related flows, response headers, and endpoint mapping all have ordering assumptions.

14
00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:55.140
When middleware is placed incorrectly, the application may skip a control, apply it too late, expose a development page, or produce inconsistent behavior that is hard to review.

15
00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.180
Production error behavior should be safe by default.

16
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:08.080
Users should not see stack traces, internal paths, framework details, raw database errors, secrets, or configuration values.

17
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:18.900
Operators still need useful diagnostic information, but that detail belongs in protected logs and monitoring systems with correlation identifiers, access control, and retention policy.

18
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.720
Separate what the user sees from what an authorized operator investigates.

19
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.960
Before release, review startup and program configuration as security code.

20
00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:38.500
Remove unnecessary diagnostic endpoints, debug features, sample routes, broad permissions, unused services, and development-only assumptions.

21
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:50.280
Confirm reverse proxy behavior, HTTPS expectations, forwarded header handling, host filtering if relevant, static file exposure, and environment-specific settings.

22
00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:55.020
The release configuration should be boring, explicit, and explainable.
